# Phase 076 — Scaffold projects: `rb new`, root discovery, and a project-aware `rb test`

## Finding reproduced

```
$ mkdir -p target/tmp/repro && cd target/tmp/repro
$ ../../../target/debug/rb new hello
Redblue v0.1.0 - A programming language as readable as plain English
...
  rb help        Show this help
exit=1
$ ls -A
(nothing)
```

`rb new hello` printed the generic help text and exited 1 without creating
anything. Bare `rb test` had no notion of a project either: `run_test(None)`
called `testing::run_all_tests()`, which always walked the literal `tests/`
directory of the working directory (`src/testing/mod.rs`). Nothing walked up to
a manifest, so a command run from `src/` could not find the project's tests.

## What changed

| File | Lines | What |
|---|---|---|
| `src/project.rs` | +191 (new) | `find_project_root` walks up to the nearest `redblue.manifest`; `create_project` writes `src/`, `tests/`, `src/main.rb`, `tests/main_test.rb` and a manifest in phase-044's schema; `check_manifest` parses it with `Manifest::parse` and refuses a directory with no manifest; `tests_dir` names the project's suite |
| `src/project.rs` | +30 −8 | `create_project` inspects the root with `symlink_metadata` and refuses a symbolic link in the project's place, including a dangling one. Following it would write the scaffold outside `parent`, the same escape a path separator would allow |
| `src/project.rs` | +24 −2 | `has_manifest` accepts only a regular `redblue.manifest`, so a manifest symlink cannot make an unrelated tree a project; `check_manifest` refuses a symlink or a non-regular manifest, naming the file, before reading it |
| `src/lib.rs` | +51 −5 | `rb new <name>` command (bare `rb new` is a usage error); bare `rb test` discovers the project root, validates its manifest and runs the root's `tests/`; a failing project suite now finishes with the same failure detail (`test name`, `FAILED`, location, message) the explicit-path form prints instead of only a count; `rb test <path>` still runs exactly the named file, and its failure location now names that file instead of `inline`; help text lists `rb new` |
| `src/testing/mod.rs` | +69 −? | `run_tests_in(dir)` is the directory-scoped runner; `run_all_tests()` now calls it with `tests/`, so the existing suite is byte-identical, and an empty directory keeps returning a zero-count result; the command layer owns the "No Redblue test files" rejection; `collect_test_files` propagates a real `read_dir` error instead of silently reporting success, skips directory symlinks so a link cycle cannot recurse, and refuses a symlinked root before `read_dir` follows it |
| `src/testing/harness.rs` | +13 −4 | `TestHarness::run_file` accepts any path-like value, so a discovered `PathBuf` is run without a lossy string conversion, and records that path as the failure location instead of labelling every file `inline`; a whole-file parse error is recorded even when the file has no `test` block, while marker-style bodies keep owning their own errors |
| `src/manifest.rs` | +20 −18 | `is_module_name` is the single name rule shared by the manifest parser and `rb new`, so the two validators cannot drift |
| `tests/discovery_test.rs` | +595 | project tests driving the `rb` binary in `target/tmp/`, beside the existing discovery tests, including the symlink-cycle, empty-library-suite, missing-manifest and explicit-path-failure contracts |
| `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs` | +1 | `resume phase-076:` joins the in-flight delivery-subject rule, so the pipeline's re-created delivery commit is acknowledged by subject rather than by a SHA that goes stale |
| `redblue.manifest` | +2 (new) | the checkout is now a project, so bare `rb test` from the repository root runs the shipped `tests/` suite instead of failing with "No Redblue project found here" |

The manifest `rb new` writes is exactly phase-044's schema and nothing more:

```text
name hello
version 0.1.0
```

No dependency, lockfile or second schema is invented. `rb new` writes the
manifest last, so a half-written layout is not discoverable as a project.

## Round 2 review fixes

| Finding | Fix |
|---|---|
| bare `rb test` exited 0 after running zero tests | `run_test(None)` now rejects a zero-count result with `No tests were defined in '<dir>'`, after the existing no-`.rb`-files guard |
| whole-file lex/parse failure reported `Location: unknown` | `TestHarness` records the parse error against `source_name` and preserves the error's span line |
| `collect_test_files` recursed without a depth bound | the walk carries a depth counter and refuses a tree deeper than 64 levels instead of overflowing the stack |
| a failed scaffold write left directories that blocked a retry | `create_project` removes the partial layout on error, keeping a pre-existing empty root while removing a root the attempt created |

## Round 3 review fixes

| Finding | Fix |
|---|---|
| `rb test <file>` still exited 0 when the file defined no tests | both forms now call `reject_zero_test_run`, so `rb test empty.rb` fails with `No tests were defined`; `edge_rb_test_with_a_path_that_defines_no_tests_fails` pins it |
| a per-file error in `run_tests_in` was labelled `Location: unknown` | `run_tests_in` calls `TestHarness::add_source_error_at(path, error)`, which records the path and the error's span; `edge_run_tests_in_names_an_unreadable_file` pins it |
| the depth bound allowed one more level than documented | the walk refuses `depth >= MAX_TEST_DIR_DEPTH` before opening that directory, and the doc/message now state the bound exactly |
| the scaffold test existed but never ran the generated entry point | `rb_new_scaffolds_a_project_that_tests_green` now runs `rb run src/main.rb` and asserts it prints `Hello from hello` |

## Round 4 resume fixes

| Finding | Fix |
|---|---|
| `rb new` followed a symbolic link in the project's place, so an empty or dangling link let the scaffold write outside the working directory | `create_project` reads `symlink_metadata` first and refuses any symbolic link, naming the link, before writing anything; `edge_rb_new_refuses_a_symlinked_directory_in_place`, `edge_rb_new_refuses_a_dangling_symlink` and `edge_create_project_refuses_a_symlinked_root` pin the command and library boundaries |

## Round 5 review fixes

| Finding | Fix |
|---|---|
| the zero-execution guard checked `total`, but a skip increments it, so a skip-only suite exited 0 having run no test | `reject_zero_test_run` now checks `passed + failed == 0`, regardless of skipped markers; `edge_rb_test_that_only_skips_fails` pins both `rb test` and `rb test <file>` |
| a whole-file lex/parse error was recorded only when a literal `test ` line was present, so a syntax error in a file with no test block was hidden behind `No tests were defined` | `run_test_blocks` records the error unless the file uses the comment-marker convention, whose bodies the marker scan reports individually; `edge_rb_test_reports_a_syntax_error_without_a_test_block` pins the file and line |
| discovery opened a caller-supplied root through a symlink because only entries below it were inspected | `collect_test_files` reads `symlink_metadata` for the root and refuses a symbolic link before `read_dir`; `edge_discovery_refuses_a_symlinked_root` pins it |
| the gate row reported a count that did not match the change set | the fresh `cargo test --all-targets` run reports 2962 passed, recorded below |

## Round 6 review fixes

| Finding | Fix |
|---|---|
| `symlink_metadata("tests/")` follows the final link because of the trailing separator, so the root guard never fired for the one caller that supplies one | the root is rebuilt from its path components before inspection, dropping trailing separators, and `edge_discovery_refuses_a_symlinked_root_with_a_trailing_separator` pins the exact `tests/` form |
| the zero-test message said "No tests were defined" even when the suite defined tests and skipped all of them | `reject_zero_test_run` distinguishes the two states and reports `No tests were run in '<dir>' (N skipped)` for an all-skipped suite |
| a benchmark body's lex/parse/runtime error was discarded, so the zero-execution guard replaced it with "No tests were defined" | `run_benchmark` records the first failure as a test failure named after the benchmark, and `edge_rb_test_reports_a_failing_benchmark` pins the command output |

## Round 7 review fixes

| Finding | Fix |
|---|---|
| a successful `// bench` recorded nothing, so the zero-execution guard rejected a file or project whose only test was a passing benchmark | `run_benchmark` calls `results.add_pass()` once the measured loop completes, and `edge_rb_test_reports_a_passing_benchmark` pins both `rb test <file>` and bare `rb test` exiting 0 |
| bare `rb test` walked the suite twice: `find_test_files` for the emptiness check, then again inside `run_tests_in` | `run_test_files(&[PathBuf])` runs an already-collected listing; `run_tests_in` collects once and calls it, and `run_test(None)` passes the listing it checked, so each project run walks the tree once |

